Column I is labeled "Accrued Interest". Excel's ACCRINT function calculates this from the issue date, first interest date, settlement date, coupon rate, par value and frequency.

A simpler and more exact approach uses the coupon dates already in row 2: the period runs from the last payment date (M2) to the next one (L2). Accrued interest is then the per-period coupon (B2*H2/100/G2) times the fraction of the period elapsed since the last payment, (D2-M2)/(L2-M2). That fraction is an actual/actual calculation, which matches the basis 1 used in the YIELD and PRICE formulas.

